\details{
The function runs in a fresh R session (\code{Rscript --vanilla}): it cannot
see your global variables. Pass data through \code{args} and list the
packages it needs in \code{packages}. Inside the function,
\code{termr_progress(value, message)} reports progress on a channel of its
own, so anything the function prints to stdout or stderr is free for
\code{on_stdout} / \code{on_stderr} (\code{message()} goes to stderr).

\code{run_process(command, args)} starts a program \emph{without a shell}: the
command and its arguments are separate, so nothing is interpolated or
interpreted. (To use a shell on purpose, run it yourself:
\code{run_process("sh", c("-c", script))}.) It completes with the exit status
as \code{result}; a non-zero status counts as failure.

Events (from the owner widget, or the screen), with \code{event$data$worker}:
\code{"worker.started"}, \code{"worker.progress"} (\code{value}, \code{message}),
\code{"worker.stdout"} / \code{"worker.stderr"} (\code{line}), \code{"worker.completed"}
(\code{result}), \code{"worker.failed"} (\code{error}, \code{timed_out}, \code{status}),
\code{"worker.cancelled"}. Output lines are stripped of control characters;
at most 200 lines are delivered per event-loop tick.

Workers stop when \code{cancel()} is called, when their owner widget is
removed, when \code{timeout} seconds have passed, and when the app exits. A
stopping worker is interrupted first (Unix) and killed, with its child
processes, if it does not end within a short grace period.

Temporary files (the job, the result and the progress file) have unique
names and are removed when the worker ends, however it ends.

The job (the function with its closure environment, and \code{args}) is
serialized to a temporary file, and workers and programs inherit the app's
environment variables: avoid putting secrets into worker closures or
arguments unnecessarily.

There is no worker pool: start as many workers as you need; each is an
independent process. This API is \strong{experimental}.
